---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { pramanaCommentary } from "../lib/pramana";
const quotes = await getCollection("quotes");
const checked = [...new Set(quotes.map((q) => q.data.checkedAt))];
---
<Base title="Sources and licences" description="Primary texts, editions and references behind Vyākaraṇa Labs.">
  <div class="page-narrow prose">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Sources and licences</h1>
      <p class="lead">Every rule on this site is cited by number, and every quoted passage was checked against a published text before it went live.</p>
    </header>

    <h2>Primary texts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pāṇini, Aṣṭādhyāyī</strong> (c. 5th–4th century BCE). Sūtra text and numbering are the standard ones. Citation links go to <a href={pramanaCommentary.astadhyayi} rel="external">Pramāṇa</a>, whose digital text comes from GRETIL (Kāśikā edition of Aryendra Sharma, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0). This site does not copy that digital edition.</li>
      <li><strong>Varadarāja, Laghusiddhāntakaumudī</strong>. Course order, LSK numbers and the vṛtti shown on every folio.</li>
      <li><strong>Bhaṭṭoji Dīkṣita, Siddhāntakaumudī</strong>. SK numbers for sūtras that the Laghusiddhāntakaumudī leaves out, and one traditional verse.</li>
      <li><strong>Patañjali, Vyākaraṇa-Mahābhāṣya</strong>, chiefly the opening Paspaśāhnika. <a href={pramanaCommentary.mahabhasya} rel="external">Catalogue entry on Pramāṇa</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Kātyāyana, Vārttikas</strong>, as transmitted in the Mahābhāṣya.</li>
      <li><strong>Jayāditya and Vāmana, Kāśikāvṛtti</strong>. <a href={pramanaCommentary.kasika} rel="external">Catalogue entry on Pramāṇa</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Bhartṛhari, Vākyapadīya</strong>, Brahmakāṇḍa, cited by kāṇḍa and verse in sequential numbering. <a href={pramanaCommentary.darsana} rel="external">Vyākaraṇadarśana on Pramāṇa</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Digital texts used</h2>
    <p>
      Sūtra texts, word splits (padaccheda), carried-over words (anuvṛtti), headings (adhikāra), LSK and SK numbers and Varadarāja's
      vṛtti come from the open dataset behind <a href="https://ashtadhyayi.com/" rel="external">ashtadhyayi.com</a>
      (<a href="https://github.com/ashtadhyayi-com/data" rel="external">github.com/ashtadhyayi-com/data</a>), which its maintainers
      offer for reuse with credit. Our gratitude to them. The same dataset's Mahābhāṣya, Kāśikā, Siddhāntakaumudī and
      Vākyapadīya texts were used to check quoted passages. Two scripts in this project's repository re-check every sūtra and
      quotation against it before release.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/prakriya/">Derivation</a> tool runs <a href="https://github.com/ambuda-org/vidyut" rel="external">vidyut-prakriyā</a>,
      Ambuda's open implementation of the Aṣṭādhyāyī (MIT licence), compiled to WebAssembly and run in your browser. Its
      derivations are its own; the course's lessons and the sandhi tool use this project's engine, which is checked against
      the texts above.
    </p>
    <p>
      Editions differ in places. Where they do and it matters to a lesson, the lesson says so: for example, the
      Laghusiddhāntakaumudī's list of internal efforts under 1.1.9, and Vākyapadīya verse numbers, which vary between editions.
    </p>

    <h2>Texts quoted were checked against</h2>
    <ul>{checked.map((u) => <li><a href={u} rel="external">{new URL(u).hostname}{new URL(u).pathname}</a></li>)}</ul>

    <h2>Further reading on Pramāṇa</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={pramanaCommentary.vyakarana} rel="external">Grammar (Vyākaraṇa)</a>: the full catalogue of grammatical literature.</li>
      <li><a href={pramanaCommentary.linganusasana} rel="external">Hemacandra's Liṅgānuśāsana</a>: a treatise on grammatical gender, which the noun modules draw on.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is original here</h2>
    <p>Lesson prose, plain-English renderings of sūtras, translations of quoted passages, exercises and software are original to Vyākaraṇa Labs. The source texts themselves are ancient and in the public domain.</p>

    <h2>Known gaps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Pramāṇa's Aṣṭādhyāyī page for pāda 1.1 currently stops at 1.1.45. For later sūtras in that pāda, citation links go to the pāda page.</li>
      <li>Pramāṇa prints 6.1.125 as <i>plutapragṛhyā aci</i>, without <i>nityam</i>, which the Kāśikā and the Siddhāntakaumudī read.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
